From f6188d22ddb55219fe6f31455def74520cc37b46 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Deniz Kokden <koekdendeniz@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 2 Nov 2026 14:12:48 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 062/109] ARM: sprd: SC7730 CPU clock and SC2723 DCDC_ARM
 regulator

Glue for CPU frequency scaling with cpufreq-dt, after the vendor's
cpufreq-scx35.c, clk-sc.c and sc2723-regulator_dt.c:

- clk-cpu: the Cortex-A7 cluster clock. CA7_CKG_CFG selects TDPLL
  (768 MHz) or MPLL; MPLL = 26 MHz * (NINT + KINT / 2^20) in MPLL_CFG1.
  A rate change parks the core on TDPLL, reprograms MPLL, waits 50 us
  per 100 MHz (at least 100 us, there is no lock bit) and switches back;
  768 MHz runs from TDPLL itself. AXI stays at core/3 up to 1.5 GHz and
  goes to core/4 above. The rate is reported to the kHz so that KINT's
  odd Hz still match the OPP table.
- sc2723-dcdc-arm: the cluster supply. In the PMIC's "non-linear" mode
  (MIXED_CTRL0 bit 14 clear, checked at probe) DCDC_ARM_ADI holds a base
  voltage index and a 3.125 mV trim; selectors are linear from 650 mV
  and mapped onto base + trim. It moves in 25 mV steps like the vendor,
  5 us per step when rising. Doing that in the driver rather than with
  regulator-max-step-microvolt avoids the core's stepped minimum above
  the target maximum when lowering, which failed with -EWOULDBLOCK.
  The rail itself is never switched.

Tested on the Galaxy Tab E 9.6 (SM-T560), chip ID 0x8730C001: every
change between 768, 1000, 1200 and 1300 MHz lands on the measured core
clock (busy loop) and, through the PMIC ADC on channel 13, on the
expected voltage; 13 changes with four-core sha256 load against a
reference showed no error.

Assisted-by: Claude Opus 5.5
Signed-off-by: Deniz Kokden <koekdendeniz@gmail.com>
